# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Problem

`Thread.Abort()` and `ThreadAbortException` are **not supported in .NET 8** — they throw `PlatformNotSupportedException`. You must replace this with cooperative cancellation.

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Task _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts == null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait for graceful exit with timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (_workerTask != null)
        {
            var completed = await Task.WhenAny(
                _workerTask,
                Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout));

            if (completed != _workerTask)
            {
                // Worker didn't stop in time — log warning; cannot force-kill
                Log.Warning("Worker did not stop within timeout");
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts?.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2));
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Details |
|---------|---------|
| **`Thread.Abort()` throws at runtime** | It compiles but throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()` gone** | No equivalent — the entire abort mechanism is removed. |
| **Blocking calls ignore cancellation** | If `GetNextItem()` blocks on I/O or a `BlockingCollection`, the token won't interrupt it. You must pass `CancellationToken` into blocking APIs (e.g., `BlockingCollection.Take(token)`). |
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no safe way to force-kill a thread. Design all work to be cancellation-aware. |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A flag alone can't unblock a thread stuck in a wait/sleep. `CancellationToken` integrates with `WaitHandle`, `Task.Delay`, and async I/O. |
| **Fire-and-forget risk** | Using `Task.Run` without storing/awaiting the task silently swallows exceptions. Always store and observe the task. |

## Migration Checklist

1. Replace `Thread` + `volatile bool` → `Task` + `CancellationTokenSource`
2. Replace `Thread.Abort()` → `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()`
3. Replace `catch (ThreadAbortException)` → `catch (OperationCanceledException)`
4. Remove `Thread.ResetAbort()` entirely
5. Pass `CancellationToken` into **all** blocking/async calls
6. Replace `Thread.Sleep()` → `await Task.Delay(ms, token)`
7. Implement `IDisposable` to clean up the `CancellationTokenSource`

## If You Can't Make Everything Async

```csharp
// Synchronous version using a dedicated thread (still cooperative)
_workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
_workerThread.IsBackground = true;
_workerThread.Start();

// In Stop():
_cts.Cancel();
_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
// If it doesn't join, you're stuck — redesign the blocking call.
```

The fundamental shift: .NET 8 requires you to **design for cancellation** rather than relying on external thread termination.
